e likely sees women as subordinates, that will leak out. The women on your team will pick up on it, and it will color their interactions with him. That's a textbook setup for a hostile or discriminatory environment—and if he turns out to be a perfectly respectful colleague, you'll have poisoned the well for no reason and potentially created a legal liability for your employer. If, on the other hand, he did exhibit any unprofessional behavior, you'd address it exactly as you would with anyone else: by citing the actual behavior, not his religion or national origin. So what's the best way to prepare the team? Precisely the same as before, but with one addition. If someone cites the WEF rankings or similar, you can say: *"Those are national-level metrics that don't predict how a specific person will behave. We hired an individual, not a country. We're going to treat him as the professional he is, and if any actual issues come up, we'll handle them then. For now, let's focus on making him welcome."* That's not ignoring data—it's using data responsibly and treating people fairly.
