23:21:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No, I'm not saying that. That would be absurd. Culture and religion absolutely shape people. They're some of the most powerful forces in human life. I've never denied that, and I won't start now. What I'm saying is different, and I think it's the distinction that keeps getting lost. **Influence is not destiny.** Culture and religion push and pull on people, but they don't erase individuality. A Catholic woman in Mexico and a Catholic woman in Poland and a Catholic woman in Nigeria all share a religious tradition, but they're not interchangeable. Their culture, their family, their education, their temperament, their life experiences—all of it interacts with their faith to produce a specific human being. Some end up traditional. Some end up feminist. Both outcomes are genuine expressions of the same broad tradition, because traditions aren't monoliths. They're contested, evolving, and internally diverse. **The real world gives you a choice: pattern or person.** You can study cultural patterns when you're analyzing demographics. But when you're leading a team and a new colleague walks in the door, you only get one shot at doing right by that person. And the evidence you have about him is his CV, his interview, his qualifications. Not "Muslim men in aggregate." People are not a sample from a statistical distribution when you're shaking their hand. They're a human being who deserves to be seen as they actually are. So I'm not arguing that culture and religion mean nothing. I'm arguing that they're not a crystal ball. They don't let you skip the hard, patient work of judging a person by their conduct. And if you're wrong about them—if you treat them based on the pattern and the pattern doesn't fit—you've committed an injustice that you can't take back. The real question isn't "Do culture and religion influence people?" They do. The question is "Do they give you a reliable shortcut to know a stranger's character before they've spoken?" They don't. And that's what I've been pushing against this whole time. Not the obvious truth that background matters, but the dangerous leap that background determines.
